      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-225238",
      ":question/problem": "Consider a population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Let $I_{1}$ be a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic obtained from a sample of size $n_{1}$.\n\nSuppose we require a $98%$ confidence interval $I_{2}$ to have approximately the same length as $I_{1}$. Fill in the missing information to find the sample size $n_{2}$ we should use to get $I_{2}$. You may assume that the point estimate $p^(^)$ is the same for the two samples.\n\nYou may use the following percentage points table for the normal distribution.\n\n| $z$ | $0.95$ | $0.975$ | $0.98$ | $0.99$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\u03a6^{-1}(p)$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.054$ | $2.326$ |\n\n$n_{2}$ must be {{field-1}} then $n_{1}$ by a factor of approximately {{field-2}}.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."
    }
